\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The constitutive law $\mu (x) = x/(x{+}1)$, partitioned between the two projections identified by the coupling polynomial. Blue: the $s = +1$ projection $\mu $ (TetA, retained by general relativity). Red: the $s = -1$ projection $1 - \mu $ (TetB, removed by the torsion-free condition). At every radius, blue~$+$~red~$= 1$. The two surfaces are exact mirrors, crossing at $x = 1$ where both projections contribute equally ($\mu = 1/2$); the white ring marks this locus. The polynomial identifies which projection is which; the constitutive law determines how much each carries at every radius.}}{3}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig1}{{2}{3}{The constitutive law $\mu (x) = x/(x{+}1)$, partitioned between the two projections identified by the coupling polynomial. Blue: the $s = +1$ projection $\mu $ (TetA, retained by general relativity). Red: the $s = -1$ projection $1 - \mu $ (TetB, removed by the torsion-free condition). At every radius, blue~$+$~red~$= 1$. The two surfaces are exact mirrors, crossing at $x = 1$ where both projections contribute equally ($\mu = 1/2$); the white ring marks this locus. The polynomial identifies which projection is which; the constitutive law determines how much each carries at every radius}{figure.2}{}}
